 "notes": "Typed rebel (a revolt that held land for years). The Glyndwr Rising article could not be opened under Glyndŵr_Rising (url_not_found), but it opens as Glyndwr_Rising (no diacritic); both pages are used. The end year 1409 is the fall of Harlech; sporadic resistance lasted to 1412 and Henry V's pardon offers came in 1415-1416. The Franco-Welsh treaty of 1404-1406 (Pennal letter of 31 March 1406) gave no territory. [Fix eu-11-x, 2026-10-25] After the review: (1) the all-Wales shape ends in 1405 (it is Wales without Flintshire; the lead says 'the entirety of the country between 1404 and 1405'); the decline is dated: south-east May 1405 (Pwll Melyn; the Glyndwr Rising page says 1406), Anglesey late 1406, south Wales by 1407, Ceredigion autumn 1408, Gwynedd and Conwy February 1409, each mirrored in principality-of-wales (counties) or marcher-lordships (contest zones that end in 1405 and 1407); (2) Anglesey is dropped from 1407; (3) a second source page, Glyndwr Rising, is now cited. Not modelled: Carmarthen, Cardiff and Newport castles taken in 1403 (Glyndwr Rising page) and the French landing of 1405; the circle of 1400-1403 overlaps the March in about 7 H3 cells of Denbighshire and Powys. No keyframe for 1409: Harlech fell in February 1409 and the shape of 1407-1408 is the last drawn."
